      <div class="ph__warning">
        <h2>Where should Powerhouse be tested?</h2>
        <p><b>Provisional recommendation: New York City, within the New York case.</b> For the thesis’s civic/community purpose, the strongest current rationale is dependence on shared charging combined with neighborhood use. This is a research judgment, not an official ranking or a final site decision.</p>
        <p><b>The alternatives matter.</b> California leads on current market size and absolute vehicle growth. Florida leads on relative historical growth and the vehicle-to-public-port indicator. If commercial charging throughput is the primary objective, the present evidence does not justify choosing New York over them.</p>
        <p><b>The strongest counterargument:</b> NYC is already expanding curbside and municipal charging. Powerhouse must demonstrate additional community benefit compared with simply adding chargers to existing parking. High charging occupancy does not prove people will stay, use shared facilities or meet one another.</p>
        <p><b>Decision test:</b> compare matched neighborhoods in all three states for home-charging access, actual sessions and kWh, dwell time, planned competitors, grid capacity, land/operating cost, and community services requested by residents. Measure space accessible without purchase and access by walking, cycling and transit. Without those observations, a numerical “best state” score would imply certainty the data do not support.</p>
        <p class="ph__note">Basis: official AFDC comparisons above; <a href="https://www.nyc.gov/html/dot/html/pr2026/expansion-of-curbside-electric-vehicle-charging.shtml">NYC DOT access and expansion evidence</a>; Powerhouse’s stated civic hypothesis. The uniform 2040 scenario cannot select a winner because every state is assigned the same national trajectory.</p>
      </div>

      <details class="ph__details">
        <summary>2040 scenario, scope and calculation</summary>
        <div class="ph__definitions">
          <p><b>Official national outlook.</b> EIA’s AEO2026 Counterfactual Baseline, Table 39, models {million(future.total)} US light-duty vehicles in 2040: {million(future.bev)} battery EVs and {million(future.phev)} plug-in hybrids. It is a policy-dependent scenario, not a prediction.</p>
          <p><b>State calculation.</b> Multiply each state’s 2025 AFDC light-duty total by EIA’s national 2040 / 2025 stock ratio ({growth.toFixed(3)}). Apply the national 2040 shares: {pct(future.bev, future.total)} battery EV, {pct(future.phev, future.total)} plug-in hybrid, and the remaining fuel / hydrogen mix. This assumes the same national trajectory for every state; actual state policy, population and adoption can differ substantially.</p>
          <p><b>Historical scope.</b> FHWA 2015 includes automobiles, buses, trucks and motorcycles. AFDC covers light-duty vehicles and offers comparable fuel categories from 2016; it rounds counts to 100. Fuel-powered includes gasoline, diesel, conventional hybrids, flex-fuel and gaseous fuels. PHEVs are kept separate because they use both electricity and fuel. CAGR = (2025 / 2016)^(1/9) − 1.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

